  "worked_solution": "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z=0 \\\\ 0x+0y-2z=4 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3z=0 \\\\ -2z=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nStarting with the second equation $z=-2,$ we substitute into the first equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+3(-2)=0\\,\u21d2\\,x=2y+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2y+6 \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2y+6 \\\\ y \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
